Ported (behavior re-written in Rust, no code copied) to
crates/kgraf-sandbox/src/egress.rs and the Network::Proxy rule of
crates/kgraf-sandbox/src/sandbox/macos.rs (decision 0087, amended 2026-10-07).

What was taken:
- The design: a host-side HTTP proxy that decides by host name, with the
  sandbox limited to the proxy's loopback port.
- The Seatbelt rule form for that port: `(allow network-outbound (remote ip
  "localhost:PORT"))`.
- The host pattern rules: an exact name, or `*.domain` for every name below
  the domain (never the domain itself, never an IP address), with an optional
  port.
- The address classes an allowed name may not resolve to unless an address
  entry names the address: loopback, unspecified, link-local, multicast,
  broadcast, and the cloud metadata endpoints outside link-local.
- The proxy variables a command gets, with NO_PROXY emptied.

Deliberately different from upstream:
- Linux: upstream bridges the command's network namespace with socat relays
  over a unix socket bound into it. kGraf's seccomp filter refuses unix
  sockets, so the command's first process opens the listener inside its own
  namespace and passes it to kGraf (SCM_RIGHTS); no helper program.
- No SOCKS proxy, no TLS termination, no request filtering beyond the host;
  an entry without a port allows 443 and 80 only.
